-- canonical-case-start Hyperliquid L1
WITH causal_seed(payload) AS (
  VALUES ('{"chain":"Hyperliquid L1","sources":[{"id":"hl-docs","title":"Hyperliquid technical overview","publisher":"Hyperliquid","url":"https://hyperliquid.gitbook.io/hyperliquid-docs","source_role":"primary","checked_at":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"hl-team","title":"Hyperliquid core contributors","publisher":"Hyperliquid","url":"https://hyperliquid.gitbook.io/hyperliquid-docs/about-hyperliquid/core-contributors","source_role":"primary","checked_at":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"hl-genesis","title":"HYPE genesis","publisher":"Hyper Foundation","url":"https://hyperfnd.medium.com/hype-genesis-1830a4dc2e3f","source_role":"primary","checked_at":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"hl-risks","title":"Hyperliquid risk disclosures","publisher":"Hyperliquid","url":"https://hyperliquid.gitbook.io/hyperliquid-docs/risks","source_role":"primary","checked_at":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"hl-fees","title":"Hyperliquid L1 fee metrics","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://api.llama.fi/overview/fees/Hyperliquid%20L1?dataType=dailyFees","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"hl-stables","title":"Stablecoin supply by chain","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://stablecoins.llama.fi/stablecoinchains","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-07-29"}],"forensic_analysis":{"version":"forensic-analysis-v1","outcome":{"label":"thriving","summary":"Hyperliquid L1 is thriving as a vertically integrated trading chain, with high observed fees and stablecoin balances centered on one exchange stack. This is a strong product outcome but not proof of a diversified neutral L1. Chain and application activity are causally coupled, so adding their metrics would double-count the same economic engine.","confidence":"high","as_of":"2026-07-29","source_refs":["hl-docs","hl-fees","hl-stables","hl-risks"]},"why":{"summary":"Observed: Hyperliquid built a purpose-specific onchain order book and perpetuals venue before generalizing the L1 through staking, spot assets and HyperEVM. It reports no venture financing, distributed HYPE heavily to users, and discloses consensus, oracle, bridge and liquidation risks. Strategic inference: vertical integration let one team optimize execution, liquidity, custody, liquidation and validator economics as a single product, creating a trader flywheel that neutral general-purpose chains struggled to match. Community distribution strengthened user alignment and reduced the usual investor-unlock narrative. The same concentration creates correlated failure: exchange liquidity, validator/oracle behavior, bridge custody, token confidence and chain reputation can all break together. Hyperliquid succeeded because it solved a demanding application first; diversification beyond that application is promising but not yet proven.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["hl-docs","hl-team","hl-genesis","hl-risks","hl-fees","hl-stables"]},"strategic_choices":[{"decision":"Build a sovereign high-performance L1 around the exchange rather than deploy on a general chain.","consequence":"Execution and liquidity could be optimized end to end, while the protocol assumed consensus, bridge and validator responsibilities.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["hl-docs","hl-risks"]},{"decision":"Use an onchain central-limit-order-book and validator-maintained oracle inputs.","consequence":"Traders receive familiar execution and transparent settlement, while oracle manipulation and liquidation coupling become protocol-critical.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["hl-docs","hl-risks"]},{"decision":"Distribute HYPE primarily to users and avoid a reported venture allocation.","consequence":"Community ownership and token loyalty became a distribution advantage, though beneficial concentration and future governance still require measurement.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["hl-genesis","hl-team"]},{"decision":"Add HyperEVM after proving the core trading product.","consequence":"External applications gain access to the chain’s users and liquidity, while the ecosystem can diversify only if those apps develop independent demand.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["hl-docs"]}],"counterfactual":{"summary":"Deploying on an established L1 or L2 would have reduced consensus and bridge responsibilities, but likely sacrificed the execution control and latency central to the product. Launching a general-purpose chain before the exchange could have created an undifferentiated cold start. A more independent validator and oracle structure would reduce correlated risk while potentially slowing coordinated product changes.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["hl-docs","hl-risks","hl-genesis"]},"watch":[{"signal":"Fees, stablecoins, retained traders and liquidity through volatile and quiet market regimes.","implication":"Durability requires activity beyond one favorable trading cycle.","source_refs":["hl-fees","hl-stables"]},{"signal":"HyperEVM applications, fees and users not attributable to HyperCore trading.","implication":"Independent demand would turn a successful appchain into a more diversified L1.","source_refs":["hl-docs"]},{"signal":"Validator, oracle and bridge concentration plus downtime or manipulation incidents.","implication":"A correlated failure would directly challenge the vertically integrated trust premium.","source_refs":["hl-risks"]}],"unknowns":[{"question":"Who beneficially controls validators, delegations and oracle influence?","resolution_trigger":"Independent operator and infrastructure mapping."},{"question":"How much revenue and user activity exists outside the core exchange?","resolution_trigger":"Application-level attribution excluding HyperCore flows."},{"question":"What loss-absorption and governance process applies after a major oracle or bridge incident?","resolution_trigger":"Published incident procedures and funded backstop terms."},{"question":"How durable is liquidity when trading volatility and token incentives fall?","resolution_trigger":"Multi-regime cohorts of deposits, makers and fees."}],"review":{"status":"current","last_reviewed_at":"2026-07-29","next_review_at":"2026-08-05","reviewer":"chaindump-research-desk"}}}')
)
UPDATE chain_facts AS facts
SET
  data = CASE facts.dimension
    WHEN 'synthesis' THEN json_set(
      facts.data,
      '$.forensic_analysis',
      json(json_extract((SELECT payload FROM causal_seed), '$.forensic_analysis'))
    )
    WHEN '_meta' THEN json_set(
      facts.data,
      '$.forensic_analysis_version', 'forensic-analysis-v1',
      '$.last_reviewed', '2026-07-29',
      '$.next_review_at',
      json_extract(
        (SELECT payload FROM causal_seed),
        '$.forensic_analysis.review.next_review_at'
      )
    )
    ELSE facts.data
  END
  ,
  sources = CASE
    WHEN facts.dimension = 'synthesis' THEN (
      SELECT json_group_array(json(source_json))
      FROM (
        SELECT source_json
        FROM (
          SELECT
            CASE
              WHEN new_source.value IS NULL THEN old_source.value
              ELSE json_set(
                json_patch(old_source.value, new_source.value),
                '$.checked_at',
                CASE
                  WHEN json_extract(old_source.value, '$.checked_at')
                    > json_extract(new_source.value, '$.checked_at')
                    THEN json_extract(old_source.value, '$.checked_at')
                  ELSE COALESCE(
                    json_extract(new_source.value, '$.checked_at'),
                    json_extract(old_source.value, '$.checked_at')
                  )
                END
              )
            END AS source_json,
            old_source.key AS position
          FROM json_each(COALESCE(facts.sources, '[]')) AS old_source
          LEFT JOIN json_each(
            json_extract((SELECT payload FROM causal_seed), '$.sources')
          ) AS new_source
            ON json_extract(new_source.value, '$.url')
              = json_extract(old_source.value, '$.url')
          WHERE json_extract(old_source.value, '$.url') IS NULL
            OR old_source.key = (
              SELECT MIN(candidate.key)
              FROM json_each(COALESCE(facts.sources, '[]')) AS candidate
              WHERE json_extract(candidate.value, '$.url')
                = json_extract(old_source.value, '$.url')
            )
          UNION ALL
          SELECT new_source.value AS source_json, 10000 + new_source.key AS position
          FROM json_each(
            json_extract((SELECT payload FROM causal_seed), '$.sources')
          ) AS new_source
          WHERE NOT EXISTS (
            SELECT 1
            FROM json_each(COALESCE(facts.sources, '[]')) AS existing
            WHERE json_extract(existing.value, '$.url')
              = json_extract(new_source.value, '$.url')
          )
        )
        ORDER BY position
      )
    )
    ELSE facts.sources
  END
  ,
  updated_at = '2026-07-29'
WHERE facts.chain = json_extract((SELECT payload FROM causal_seed), '$.chain')
  AND facts.dimension IN ('synthesis', '_meta');
-- canonical-case-end Hyperliquid L1

-- canonical-case-start Mantle
WITH causal_seed(payload) AS (
  VALUES ('{"chain":"Mantle","sources":[{"id":"mnt-launch","title":"Mantle mainnet launch recap","publisher":"Mantle","url":"https://www.mantle.xyz/blog/community/mainnet-launch-event-roundups-more","source_role":"primary","checked_at":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"mnt-l2beat","title":"Mantle risk analysis","publisher":"L2BEAT","url":"https://l2beat.com/scaling/projects/mantle","source_role":"independent-research","checked_at":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"mnt-tvl","title":"Mantle historical TVL","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://api.llama.fi/v2/historicalChainTvl/Mantle","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"mnt-fees","title":"Mantle fee metrics","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://api.llama.fi/overview/fees/Mantle?dataType=dailyFees","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"mnt-cg","title":"MNT market data","publisher":"CoinGecko","url":"https://api.coingecko.com/api/v3/coins/mantle","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-07-29"}],"forensic_analysis":{"version":"forensic-analysis-v1","outcome":{"label":"declining","summary":"Mantle is a live but declining Ethereum L2 in the reviewed snapshot: TVL is roughly 91% below an April 2026 peak and MNT is materially below its high, while the network still produces fees and maintains a product surface. A governance-linked capital base and launch programs created liquidity, but current retention is not yet a durable moat.","confidence":"high","as_of":"2026-07-29","source_refs":["mnt-tvl","mnt-fees","mnt-cg","mnt-l2beat"]},"why":{"summary":"Observed: Mantle launched an EVM rollup with a governance-linked ecosystem fund and liquidity-centered narrative, reached a recent TVL peak, then lost roughly nine-tenths of that capital within months. L2BEAT records Stage 0 trust assumptions involving sequencing, upgrades, validation and data availability. Strategic inference: treasury scale, MNT incentives and EVM familiarity accelerated capital formation, but the chain entered a crowded L2 market where Base owns consumer distribution and Arbitrum owns deeper established DeFi. Incentive-responsive liquidity can create impressive peaks without retained users or fee-paying applications. Mantle is not dead because the chain remains operational and earns fees; it is declining because capital exited faster than a differentiated application or distribution loop replaced it. The unresolved question is whether the broader Mantle asset ecosystem creates recurring network use or merely rotates treasury-supported liquidity.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["mnt-launch","mnt-l2beat","mnt-tvl","mnt-fees","mnt-cg"]},"strategic_choices":[{"decision":"Launch with a large governance-approved ecosystem capital pool.","consequence":"Developers and liquidity gained immediate funding, while allocation concentration and subsidy dependence became central lifecycle risks.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["mnt-launch"]},{"decision":"Position Mantle as a liquidity-focused EVM rollup.","consequence":"Ethereum compatibility lowered migration friction, but product differentiation remained weak against other EVM L2s.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["mnt-launch","mnt-l2beat"]},{"decision":"Use MNT as both network gas and ecosystem incentive asset.","consequence":"Usage and treasury programs can support token demand, while declining price and rewards can jointly accelerate capital exit.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["mnt-cg","mnt-launch"]},{"decision":"Operate under Stage 0 rollup trust assumptions while scaling the ecosystem.","consequence":"The team can upgrade and coordinate quickly, but users depend materially on current sequencer, validation and governance controls.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["mnt-l2beat"]}],"counterfactual":{"summary":"A smaller capped incentive program tied to retained fees and users could have tested organic demand before large liquidity accumulated. A distinctive first-party application or distribution partner might have created a use loop that generic EVM compatibility did not. Moving faster toward stronger rollup trust guarantees could reduce risk, but alone would not create demand.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["mnt-launch","mnt-l2beat","mnt-tvl"]},"watch":[{"signal":"Thirty-, ninety- and 180-day TVL, fees, users and application retention after incentive changes.","implication":"Stabilization in paid cohorts supports recovery; continued outflows move the chain toward failure.","source_refs":["mnt-tvl","mnt-fees"]},{"signal":"Stage 1 milestones, proof availability, upgrade delays and sequencer controls.","implication":"Reduced unilateral trust assumptions improve safety but must be verified in production.","source_refs":["mnt-l2beat"]},{"signal":"Treasury deployment by program and resulting retained fees or users.","implication":"Capital allocation is successful only when measurable demand survives funding.","source_refs":["mnt-launch"]}],"unknowns":[{"question":"What caused the April-to-July TVL collapse by protocol and asset?","resolution_trigger":"Protocol-level net-flow and incentive-event decomposition."},{"question":"How much current activity remains incentive-dependent?","resolution_trigger":"Cohorts around reward starts, cuts and expirations."},{"question":"What is the current liquid and committed governance treasury?","resolution_trigger":"Audited treasury inventory with encumbrances."},{"question":"Which applications generate repeat fees independent of Mantle-controlled programs?","resolution_trigger":"Application revenue and user-retention attribution."}],"review":{"status":"current","last_reviewed_at":"2026-07-29","next_review_at":"2026-08-05","reviewer":"chaindump-research-desk"}}}')
)
